> is z holomorphic?
  ucas: holomorphic z
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  entire: holomorphic on the whole plane
> the modulus of z
  ucas: modulus z
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  note: z = x + iy (real and imaginary parts real)
  note: i is the imaginary unit; x, y are taken as real
|x + i*y| = sqrt(x^2 + y^2)
  check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
> real and imaginary parts of z
  ucas: complex z
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  note: z = x + iy (real and imaginary parts real)
  note: i is the imaginary unit; x, y are taken as real
x + i*y = x + i*y
  Re = x,  Im = y
  check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points
> what is x and y
  note: "and"/"or" between numbers: read as bitwise (& and |), not logical (&& and ||) (say "bitwise and" or "logical and")
  ucas: it(x, y) = x & y
  ucas: show it
  note: x has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
  note: y has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
it(x: int, y: int) = x & y
  -> int
> show 3 and 5
_() = 1
  -> bool
> sign of -3
  ucas: sign(-3)
-1
